`crimes context src/Card.tsx` adds three things the scan JSON didn't carry: no keyed agent guidance matched, **no test file exists** (all three findings score `test_gap: 1.0`), and the file is cold — 2 commits in 90d, 1 author, 0 importers. So this is a safe, unobserved refactor: nothing imports `Card`, and nothing will catch a regression either.

One thing reading the file surfaces that neither the scan nor the context output states: the layout is *already* broken at every viewport. The container is `width: 800` and its two children are `720` + `240` = 960px. The responsive finding isn't just "fragile on mobile" — the fixed widths are internally inconsistent today.

## Proposed refactor

```tsx
import styles from "./Card.module.css";

export function Card({
  children,
  onSelect,
  label,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onSelect: () => void;
  label: string;
}) {
  return (
    <button type="button" className={styles.card} onClick={onSelect} aria-label={label}>
      <div className={styles.body}>{children}</div>
      <div className={styles.sidebar}>sidebar</div>
    </button>
  );
}
```

```css
/* Card.module.css */
.card {
  display: flex;
  gap: var(--space-3);
  width: 100%;
  max-width: var(--size-card-max);   /* was 800px */
  padding: var(--space-3);
  background: var(--color-brand);     /* was #ff00aa */
  color: var(--color-ink);            /* was #0a0a0a */
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-1);
  text-align: left;
  font: inherit;
}
.card:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-focus); outline-offset: 2px; }

.body    { flex: 1 1 auto; font-size: var(--font-size-md); }
.sidebar { flex: 0 0 clamp(12rem, 25%, 15rem); color: var(--color-ink-muted); }

@container (max-width: 40rem) {
  .card { flex-direction: column; }
  .sidebar { flex-basis: auto; }
}
```

And the token scale has to grow — the current config only defines three colors, none of which cover border, muted text, focus ring, or shadow:

```ts
// tailwind.config.ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#666666",   // was inline #666666 / #333333
      border: "#cccccc",        // was inline #cccccc
      focus: "#1a73e8",         // was inline #1a73e8
    },
    boxShadow: { 1: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16)" },
  },
};
```

## Change → finding

**`accessible_interaction_risk` (crime_00001, medium, confidence 0.85)** — `<div onClick>` at line 22 becomes a real `<button type="button">` with an `aria-label` from a new required prop. This takes the `suggested_actions` first branch ("use a `<button>`") rather than the `role="button"` + `tabIndex={0}` fallback, which is the right call here because the element has no competing semantics — the fallback exists for cases where you can't change the tag. Free consequences: keyboard focus, Enter/Space activation, and the `:focus-visible` ring, which the original had an `outlineColor` for but no reachable focus state to apply it to. Note this is the one **API-breaking** change in the set — `Card` gains `onSelect` and `label`. With 0 importers that costs nothing today; it would not be free later.

**`design_token_escape` (crime_00002, low, confidence 0.75)** — all 9 inline literals move out of `style={{}}` into CSS custom properties backed by the Tailwind theme. The detector's framing is the point: an agent extending this file copies `#ff00aa` forward and silently forks the palette. The important half of this fix is the config change, not the component change — mapping `#0a0a0a` → `ink` is mechanical, but `#cccccc`, `#666666`, `#1a73e8`, and the shadow have **no token to escape to**. Swapping them for the three existing colors would be wrong; they need new named roles first. `#ff00aa` is a judgment call I'd flag rather than decide: it's not a near-miss on `brand: #0050ff`, so it's either an intentional accent that deserves its own token or fixture noise.

**`responsive_fragility` (crime_00003, low, confidence 0.65)** — `width: 800` → `width: 100%` + `max-width`; `width: 720` and `width: 240` → flex ratios; `fontSize: 18` → a rem-based token. The 720+240-inside-800 overflow gets fixed as a side effect of dropping fixed children widths. I've used a **container query** rather than the `@media` the suggested action mentions, matching the finding's own `fix_shape` ("drive layout from container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries") — a card is a component, not a page, so it should respond to its slot rather than the viewport. If the build can't target `@container`, `@media (max-width: 40rem)` is the equivalent fallback and will also satisfy the detector's "no `@media` in file" evidence line.

## Sequencing and verification

Land the token config first, then the CSS module, then the component API change — that way the `design_token_escape` fix is reviewable on its own and the breaking prop change is isolated in one commit.

Two caveats worth stating plainly. First, `test_gap: 1.0` on all three findings is not incidental: there's no `Card.test.tsx`, so the accessibility fix in particular ships unverified. I'd add a test asserting the rendered element is `role="button"` and reachable by keyboard *before* the refactor, so it's the thing proving the fix rather than a formality after it. Second, converting inline styles to a CSS module assumes this project has CSS-module support wired up — the fixture has only a `package.json` and a Tailwind config, so I can't confirm that from here. If it doesn't, the same refactor works with Tailwind utility classes (`w-full max-w-3xl bg-brand text-ink`), which is arguably a better fit given the config already exists; the finding-to-change mapping above is unchanged either way.

I haven't modified the fixture — it's an eval input, and editing it would move the baseline.
